From u9d8db47258b6cddc at groups.google.com  Sat Dec  4 21:00:55 2010
From: u9d8db47258b6cddc at groups.google.com (Steven Brady)
Date: Sat, 04 Dec 2010 21:00:55 +0000
Subject: Plotting results following pcount() with multiple siteCovs
Message-ID: <-ZMzb9WES3M.gY4LWr8mkRoJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/-ZMzb9WES3M/m/gY4LWr8mkRoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi All:

Can anyone recommend a defensible way to plot a raw response variable
(say abundance) against multiple site level covariates?
Alternatively, is it acceptable to plot a response variable against a
single predictor despite significance of multiple predictors in the
model?

In this case, I have abundance data recorded across sites that vary
significantly in both land cover and wetland size.  My primary
interest in this study was in the relationship between land cover and
wetland bird abundance (wetland size just happened to be a significant
covariate).  So really, I am especially interested in visually
conveying this effect of land cover, and less interested in the effect
of size.  But of course, given their respective significance, I feel
like I should convey both.

Would it be poor form to fit two follow-up models (one for each
covariate) simply for the purpose of generating two separate plots
(along with their respective fitted line)?

As a side comment, I have seen partial regression plot methods in
{car} as a solution to generating a plot against multiple covariates.
But I am not convinced that that approach would be appropriate in
cases such as this, where detection probability is significant.

Many thanks for any comments!

-Steve

